<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dorset</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dorset is a county on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> coast of south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, with its county town at Dorchester. In the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> it was the heartland of the Durotriges, whose many hillforts include Maiden Castle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hod-hill">Hod Hill</a>. It later formed part of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>, and its coast, rich in fossils, is part of the Jurassic Coast World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dorset.jpg" alt="Dorset" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saffron Blaze · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Durdle_Door_Overview.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the eighth century the kingdom was divided into shires named after their central places or peoples, such as Hampshire, Wiltshire, <b>Dorset</b> and Somerset, each under an ealdorman, with royal estates collecting food renders, and the king consulted his councillors, the witan, on law and policy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The first raid recorded in England struck Portland in <b>Dorset</b>, about 789, when three ships from Hordaland killed the king’s reeve.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1834 six farm labourers from Tolpuddle in <b>Dorset</b>, led by the Methodist preacher George Loveless, were sentenced to transportation to Australia for swearing a secret oath; a petition of some 800,000 signatures and mass demonstrations won their pardon in 1836.</blockquote><a href="/movement/labour-movement">Labour movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>T. E. Lawrence, 'Lawrence of Arabia', died six days after crashing near his <b>Dorset</b> cottage.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-19">19 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Durotriges took shape in the late Iron Age across <b>Dorset</b> and neighbouring parts of Somerset, Wiltshire and Hampshire, a landscape dominated by great multivallate hillforts.</blockquote><a href="/state/durotriges">Durotriges</a></li></ul></section>
